{"content":[{"c":["JOAB"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["1. Son of Zeruiah, the half sister of David (",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.2.16","r":"1Chr.2.16","rawR":"1Chr.2.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.2.16","value":"1Chr.2.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 2:16"},"), who, along with his brothers Abishai and Asahel, was well known for his military valor in Judah (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.2.18","r":"2Sam.2.18","rawR":"2Sam.2.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.2.18","value":"2Sam.2.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 2:18"},"; cf. ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.26.6","r":"1Sam.26.6","rawR":"1Sam.26.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.26.6","value":"1Sam.26.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sm 26:6"},"). According to 2 Samuel, Joab rose to prominence and distinguished himself at the battle of Gibeon when Saul’s troops under Abner were vanquished (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.2.8-32","r":"2Sam.2.8-32","rawR":"2Sam.2.8-32","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.2.8-32","value":"2Sam.2.8-32"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 2:8-32"},"). Because Abner had slain Joab’s brother Asahel (v ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.2.23","r":"2Sam.2.23","rawR":"2Sam.2.23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.2.23","value":"2Sam.2.23"}],"t":"ref","v":"23"},"), Joab later killed Abner in revenge (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.3.26-30","r":"2Sam.3.26-30","rawR":"2Sam.3.26-30","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.3.26-30","value":"2Sam.3.26-30"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:26-30"},"), despite Abner’s new loyalty to David (vv ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.3.12-19","r":"2Sam.3.12-19","rawR":"2Sam.3.12-19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.3.12-19","value":"2Sam.3.12-19"}],"t":"ref","v":"12-19"},"). Possibly Joab sensed that Abner would be his rival. Nevertheless, David praised the slain commander as a prince and a great man (vv ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.3.31-39","r":"2Sam.3.31-39","rawR":"2Sam.3.31-39","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.3.31-39","value":"2Sam.3.31-39"}],"t":"ref","v":"31-39"},") and set a curse on the house of Joab for his insubordination (vv ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.3.26-29,39","r":"2Sam.3.26-29,39","rawR":"2Sam.3.26-29,39","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.3.26-29,39","value":"2Sam.3.26-29,39"}],"t":"ref","v":"26-29, 39"},"). This incident illumines Joab’s sometimes unscrupulous and ruthless behavior."],"sourceClass":"list","t":"p"},{"c":["Joab spearheaded David’s siege of the Jebusite city of Jerusalem, and when David consolidated his reign there, Joab became the commander of the king’s army (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.8.16","r":"2Sam.8.16","rawR":"2Sam.8.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.8.16","value":"2Sam.8.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 8:16"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.11.1","r":"2Sam.11.1","rawR":"2Sam.11.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.11.1","value":"2Sam.11.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"11:1"},"; cf. ",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.11.6-8","r":"1Chr.11.6-8","rawR":"1Chr.11.6-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.11.6-8","value":"1Chr.11.6-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 11:6-8"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.18.15","r":"1Chr.18.15","rawR":"1Chr.18.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.18.15","value":"1Chr.18.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"18:15"},"). He suppressed a rebellion among the Syrians and Ammonites (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.10.7-14","r":"2Sam.10.7-14","rawR":"2Sam.10.7-14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.10.7-14","value":"2Sam.10.7-14"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 10:7-14"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.19.8-15","r":"1Chr.19.8-15","rawR":"1Chr.19.8-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.19.8-15","value":"1Chr.19.8-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 19:8-15"},"). At Rabbah he not only conquered the city (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.11.1-12.31","r":"2Sam.11.1-12.31","rawR":"2Sam.11.1-12.31","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.11.1-12.31","value":"2Sam.11.1-12.31"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 11–12"},") but arranged for the death of Uriah the Hittite so that David could take Uriah’s wife, Bathsheba."],"sourceClass":"list-text","t":"p"},{"c":["Joab’s loyalties to David and shrewd control of the army are seen during Absalom’s rebellion (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.15.1-37","r":"2Sam.15.1-37","rawR":"2Sam.15.1-37","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.15.1-37","value":"2Sam.15.1-37"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 15"},"). Joab suppressed the conspiracy (ch ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.18.1-33","r":"2Sam.18.1-33","rawR":"2Sam.18.1-33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.18.1-33","value":"2Sam.18.1-33"}],"t":"ref","v":"18"},"), but ignoring a direct order from David not to kill his son (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.18.5","r":"2Sam.18.5","rawR":"2Sam.18.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.18.5","value":"2Sam.18.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"18:5"},"), brutally killed him anyway (vv ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.18.10-17","r":"2Sam.18.10-17","rawR":"2Sam.18.10-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.18.10-17","value":"2Sam.18.10-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"10-17"},"). When David mourned, Joab rebuked the king, urging that a crisis with the army was imminent (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.19.5-7","r":"2Sam.19.5-7","rawR":"2Sam.19.5-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.19.5-7","value":"2Sam.19.5-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:5-7"},"). This insubordination led David to replace Joab with Amasa as commander (v ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.19.13","r":"2Sam.19.13","rawR":"2Sam.19.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.19.13","value":"2Sam.19.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"13"},"), but later, at Gibeon, Joab also killed him dishonorably (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.20.8-10","r":"2Sam.20.8-10","rawR":"2Sam.20.8-10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.20.8-10","value":"2Sam.20.8-10"}],"t":"ref","v":"20:8-10"},"). Joab’s influence in the army must have been great, since he regained his former role as military commander (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.20.23,24","r":"2Sam.20.23,24","rawR":"2Sam.20.23,24","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.20.23,24","value":"2Sam.20.23,24"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 20:23, 24:2"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.1.19","r":"1Kgs.1.19","rawR":"1Kgs.1.19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.1.19","value":"1Kgs.1.19"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 1:19"},")."],"sourceClass":"list-text","t":"p"},{"c":["At the end of David’s reign, Joab supported the conspiracy of Adonijah and Abiathar against the throne (",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.1.7","r":"1Kgs.1.7","rawR":"1Kgs.1.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.1.7","value":"1Kgs.1.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 1:7"},"). David’s distrust of him led the king to warn Solomon specifically about Joab’s repeated treacheries (",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.2.5-9","r":"1Kgs.2.5-9","rawR":"1Kgs.2.5-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.2.5-9","value":"1Kgs.2.5-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:5-9"},"). Solomon had to resolve the problem of an untrustworthy army. Therefore, upon his father’s death, Solomon pursued the conspirators Adonijah (v ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.2.23","r":"1Kgs.2.23","rawR":"1Kgs.2.23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.2.23","value":"1Kgs.2.23"}],"t":"ref","v":"23"},"), Abiathar (v ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.2.26","r":"1Kgs.2.26","rawR":"1Kgs.2.26","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.2.26","value":"1Kgs.2.26"}],"t":"ref","v":"26"},"), and Joab (v ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.2.28","r":"1Kgs.2.28","rawR":"1Kgs.2.28","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.2.28","value":"1Kgs.2.28"}],"t":"ref","v":"28"},"). Solomon’s officer Benaiah found Joab at the altar seeking refuge and killed him there (vv ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.2.28-35","r":"1Kgs.2.28-35","rawR":"1Kgs.2.28-35","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.2.28-35","value":"1Kgs.2.28-35"}],"t":"ref","v":"28-35"},"), thus cleansing Solomon’s reign from the wrongdoing of Joab."],"sourceClass":"list-text","t":"p"},{"c":["2. KJV translation (“Ataroth, the house of Joab”) of Atroth-beth-joab in ",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.2.54","r":"1Chr.2.54","rawR":"1Chr.2.54","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.2.54","value":"1Chr.2.54"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chronicles 2:54"},". ",{"c":["See"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"href":"?item=AtrothBethJoab_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"AtrothBethJoab_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"AtrothBethJoab_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Atroth-beth-joab"},"."],"sourceClass":"list","t":"p"},{"c":["3. Judahite, Seraiah’s son from the house of Kenaz and forefather of the residents of the valley of craftsmen (",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.4.14","r":"1Chr.4.14","rawR":"1Chr.4.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.4.14","value":"1Chr.4.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 4:14"},")."],"sourceClass":"list","t":"p"},{"c":["4. Forefather of a clan of Jews who returned to Palestine with Zerubbabel following the exile (",{"href":"?bref=Ezra.2.6","r":"Ezra.2.6","rawR":"Ezra.2.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ezra.2.6","value":"Ezra.2.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ezr 2:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Neh.7.11","r":"Neh.7.11","rawR":"Neh.7.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Neh.7.11","value":"Neh.7.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Neh 7:11"},")."],"sourceClass":"list","t":"p"},{"c":["5. Forefather of a family of which 219 members returned with Ezra to Palestine following the exile (",{"href":"?bref=Ezra.8.9","r":"Ezra.8.9","rawR":"Ezra.8.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ezra.8.9","value":"Ezra.8.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ezr 8:9"},"). He is perhaps identifiable with #4 above."],"sourceClass":"list","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Joab","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"1 Chr 2:16","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=1Chr.2.16","rawQueryValue":"1Chr.2.16","sourceRefExact":"1Chr.2.16"},{"anchorText":"2 Sm 2:18","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.2.18","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.2.18","sourceRefExact":"2Sam.2.18"},{"anchorText":"1 Sm 26:6","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=1Sam.26.6","rawQueryValue":"1Sam.26.6","sourceRefExact":"1Sam.26.6"},{"anchorText":"2 Sm 2:8-32","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.2.8-32","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.2.8-32","sourceRefExact":"2Sam.2.8-32"},{"anchorText":"23","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.2.23","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.2.23","sourceRefExact":"2Sam.2.23"},{"anchorText":"3:26-30","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.3.26-30","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.3.26-30","sourceRefExact":"2S